            {
              "id": "3d5d778c-65fb-388d-b0db-2c72c70b32ad",
              "name": "Potential ReDoS Vulnerability Due to RegExp() Function Argument 'a' in Application: Consider Hardcoded Regexes, Input Validation, or Regex Checking/Sanitization Libraries for Mitigation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to a Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) attack. This vulnerability occurs when the code uses a regular expression (`RegExp()`) with exponential worst-case time complexity. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by providing a malicious string that takes a long time to evaluate, causing the application to hang and consume high CPU resources, leading to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\nThe specific sink of the vulnerability is the `RegExp()` function argument 'a' which is used to construct a regular expression dynamically based on user input (`a.type`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following recommendations:\n\n1. **Avoid Dynamically Constructed Regular Expressions:** Where possible, use hardcoded regular expressions instead of dynamically constructing them. This allows you to control the complexity of the regular expressions and ensure they do not have exponential worst-case time complexity.\n\n2. **Input Validation:** Validate user input before using it in a regular expression. This can help to ensure that the input does not contain characters or patterns that could lead to a ReDoS attack.\n\n3. **Use Regex Checking/Sanitization Libraries:** There are libraries available that can check a regular expression for potential ReDoS vulnerabilities and sanitize it if necessary. Consider using one of these libraries to help protect your application.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix for the provided code would be to validate the `a.type` before using it in the `RegExp()` function. Here is a simple example of how you might do this:\n\n```javascript\n// Validate a.type before using it in a regular expression\nif (typeof a.type === 'string' && /^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/.test(a.type)) {\n    e=RegExp(\"(^|\\\\.)\"+ d.join(\"\\\\.(?:.*\\\\.)?\") +\"(\\\\.|$)\");\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks that `a.type` is a string and only contains alphanumeric characters before using it in the `RegExp()` function. This helps to ensure that `a.type` does not contain any characters that could lead to a ReDoS attack.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet provided appears to be using the jQuery library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP ReDoS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
