            {
              "id": "e756f894-8031-3402-bb55-5190b0ca7803",
              "name": "Prototype Pollution Vulnerability Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPrototype Pollution is a vulnerability that occurs when the properties of JavaScript's object prototype are manipulated. The prototype is an object that is associated with every function and object by default in JavaScript, where function's properties are shared through all instances. A Prototype Pollution vulnerability can allow an attacker to manipulate these properties and potentially lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\nThe provided code snippet does not seem to contain a direct Prototype Pollution vulnerability. However, it's important to note that Prototype Pollution vulnerabilities can occur in various ways, often through the misuse of functions that can modify a prototype's properties such as `Object.assign()`, `_.merge()`, and `$.extend()`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate Prototype Pollution vulnerabilities, follow these general guidelines:\n\n- Avoid using functions that can modify an object's prototype unless absolutely necessary.\n- Always validate and sanitize input data before using it, especially if it's used in functions that can modify an object's prototype.\n- Use libraries that are not vulnerable to Prototype Pollution. If a library is vulnerable, check if there's a security patch available and apply it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs the provided code does not seem to contain a direct Prototype Pollution vulnerability, a specific fix cannot be provided. However, here's an example of how to prevent Prototype Pollution when using `Object.assign()`:\n\n```javascript\nlet target = {};\nlet source = { a: '1', __proto__: { b: '2' } };\n\nObject.keys(source).forEach(key => {\n  if (key !== '__proto__') {\n    target[key] = source[key];\n  }\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, we're copying properties from `source` to `target` while explicitly avoiding the `__proto__` property.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be a part of jQuery library. Therefore, jQuery is a required dependency for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-250: Execution with Unnecessary Privileges](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html)"
