            {
              "id": "8d1bf387-6e22-363a-adde-ec3a162b9bb9",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in 'C'\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in 'C'\" in JavaScript refers to a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability that occurs when user-controlled data is not properly sanitized before being used in the 'C' function. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users, leading to potential theft of sensitive information or other malicious activities.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability lies in the 'h' function where user-controlled data 'b' is directly used to create a style element without any sanitization or encoding. This can lead to an XSS attack if 'b' contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always sanitize and/or encode user-controlled data before using it in a function or method. This can prevent the execution of malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the provided code would be to sanitize the 'b' variable before using it in the 'h' function. This can be done using a function like `encodeURI()` or `encodeURIComponent()`. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```javascript\nfunction h(a,b){\n    var c=a.createElement(\"p\"),\n    d=a.getElementsByTagName(\"head\")[0]||a.documentElement;\n    b = encodeURIComponent(b); // sanitize user-controlled data\n    return c.innerHTML=\"x<style>\"+b+\"</style>\",\n    d.insertBefore(c.lastChild,d.firstChild)\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not seem to have any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
