            {
              "id": "484aad22-8859-3bee-bde4-154c1ebd4f34",
              "name": "Potential Code Injection Vulnerability Detected through eval() Usage",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential code injection vulnerability has been detected in the JavaScript code snippet provided. The vulnerability arises from the use of the `eval()` function, which executes a string of JavaScript code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can execute arbitrary JavaScript code, leading to a variety of attacks such as Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), data theft, or even remote code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `eval()` whenever possible. If you must use it, ensure that the input is properly sanitized and validated to prevent malicious code from being executed. Consider using safer alternatives to `eval()`, such as `JSON.parse()` for parsing JSON data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `eval()` function with `JSON.parse()`. This function only parses JSON-formatted data, and does not execute code, making it a safer alternative.\n\n```javascript\nj = JSON.parse(text);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code ('Eval Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-95",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-94",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e6192395-d0dd-35f9-b348-0a44cec31c98",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in Methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn this specific case, the vulnerability arises from the use of methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write` that are controlled by user data. These methods can potentially insert malicious scripts into the DOM, leading to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `innerHTML` method is used with user-controlled data. To fix this, you should sanitize the user input before using it with `innerHTML`. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```javascript\nelse if(typeof a===\"string\"&&!Ca.test(a)&&(c.support.leadingWhitespace||!$.test(a))&&!P[(Ba.exec(a)||[\"\",\"\"])[1].toLowerCase()]){\n    a=a.replace(Aa,\"<$1></$2>\");\n    try{\n        for(var b=0,d=this.length;b<d;b++)\n            if(this[b].nodeType===1){\n                c.cleanData(this[b].getElementsByTagName(\"*\"));\n                // sanitize the user input before using it with innerHTML\n                this[b].innerHTML=c.sanitizeHTML(a)\n            }\n    }catch(e){\n        this.empty().append(a)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the jQuery library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d89d3317-2019-37a0-9799-b10abed4776b",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability in Dynamic Execution with Eval and Function\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in dynamic execution with `eval` and `Function` in JavaScript is a serious security issue. It arises when user input is directly passed into `eval()` or `Function()` functions. These functions execute the provided string as JavaScript code, which can lead to arbitrary code execution if an attacker can control the input. This is a form of Injection vulnerability, specifically JavaScript Injection or Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).\n\nIn the provided code, the `Function()` constructor is used to dynamically generate a function. If any part of the string passed to `Function()` is controlled by user input, it could lead to a vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `eval()` and the `Function()` constructor whenever possible. If dynamic script execution is necessary, consider safer alternatives like `setTimeout` and `setInterval`, which can accept string arguments but are less prone to misuse.\n\nIf you must use `eval()` or `Function()`, ensure that user input is properly sanitized before it is passed in. This could involve escaping special characters, validating the input against a whitelist of allowed values, or using a library designed to safely encode user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code is complex and it's not clear where user input might be influencing the string passed to `Function()`. However, as a general recommendation, any user input should be sanitized before it is passed to `Function()`. \n\nFor example, if `userInput` is a variable containing user input that is being passed to `Function()`, you could sanitize it like this:\n\n```javascript\nvar sanitizedInput = userInput.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9 ]/g, \"\");\nvar dynamicFunction = new Function(sanitizedInput);\n```\n\nThis will remove any non-alphanumeric characters from the user input before it is passed to `Function()`, reducing the risk of injection attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not appear to have any external library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Injection Theory](https://owasp.org/www-community/Injection_Theory)\n- [OWASP: XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
