            {
              "id": "aec2841c-f4a4-35d0-a07b-6b8963e9fcf7",
              "name": "Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability\" is a security vulnerability in JavaScript that can occur when a regular expression (regex) is used to process user inputs. ReDoS, or Regular Expression Denial of Service, is a type of Denial of Service (DoS) attack where an attacker provides a malicious input to a regular expression that causes it to take a very long time to evaluate, effectively blocking the event loop and making the application unresponsive.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using regular expressions that can result in catastrophic backtracking, or that can take a long time to evaluate with certain inputs. If you must use regular expressions on user input, make sure to sanitize and validate the input first. You can also use a timeout to prevent regular expressions from taking too long to evaluate.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not contain a regular expression, so it's not clear where the vulnerability might be. However, if there was a vulnerable regular expression, it might be fixed like this:\n\n```javascript\n// BAD: This regex is vulnerable to ReDoS\nvar vulnerableRegex = new RegExp(userInput);\n\n// GOOD: Sanitize and validate user input before using in a regex\nvar sanitizedInput = sanitize(userInput);\nif (isValid(sanitizedInput)) {\n  var safeRegex = new RegExp(sanitizedInput);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the jQuery library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP ReDoS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "69252e3b-e21b-38d6-977c-4ba72a531317",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" in JavaScript refers to a situation where an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML string without proper validation or escaping, and sends the HTML string to a web browser. This can potentially lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where malicious scripts are injected into trusted websites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `pushStack` function is concatenating user-controlled data (`v`) directly into an HTML string which forms a part of the selector. If the user-controlled data is not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid inserting user-controlled data into HTML strings.\n2. If it's necessary to include user-controlled data, ensure that the data is properly sanitized to remove any potential malicious scripts. This can be done using output encoding libraries.\n3. Use contextual output encoding/escaping of HTML entities.\n4. Use secure methods for DOM manipulation, like `textContent` or `createTextNode`, instead of `innerHTML`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the provided code snippet could be:\n\n```javascript\npushStack:function(j,s,v){\n    var z=b();\n    b.isArray(j)?M.apply(z,j):b.merge(z,j);\n    z.prevObject=this;\n    z.context=this.context;\n    if(s===\"find\")z.selector=this.selector+(this.selector?\" \":\"\")+v;\n    else if(s)z.selector=this.selector+\".\"+s+\"(\"+encodeURIComponent(v)+\")\";\n    return z\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `encodeURIComponent` function is used to sanitize the user-controlled data `v`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the jQuery library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
