            {
              "id": "0bd56245-a493-3d60-8d7c-8c125c92bccd",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the `globalEval` function where it directly evaluates the string `j` without any sanitization or encoding. This can lead to execution of malicious scripts if `j` contains any.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `globalEval` function should be modified to avoid direct evaluation of the string. If evaluation is necessary, the string should be sanitized before evaluation. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```javascript\nglobalEval: function(j) {\n    if (j && typeof j === 'string') {\n        // Sanitize the string before evaluation\n        j = j.replace(/<script[^>]*?>.*?<\\/script>/gi, '');\n        eval(j);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be a part of jQuery library. Therefore, jQuery is required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cbd10c25-cafd-30ab-8073-a5f5acdd680e",
              "name": "Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) is a type of Denial of Service attack. Many Regular Expressions implementations may reach extreme situations that cause them to work very slowly (exponentially related to input size). An attacker can then cause a program using a Regular Expression to enter these extreme situations and then hang for a very long time.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user input is passed into a regular expression without proper sanitization. This can lead to a situation where the regular expression takes a very long time to evaluate, effectively causing a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input inside a regular expression without proper sanitization. If you must use user input in a regular expression, make sure to sanitize the input to ensure it does not cause the regular expression to enter a state that would take a long time to evaluate.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not seem to contain a regular expression, so it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, here is a general example of how to sanitize user input before using it in a regular expression:\n\n```javascript\nlet userInput = getUserInput();\nlet sanitizedInput = userInput.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '');\nlet regex = new RegExp(sanitizedInput);\n```\n\nIn this example, `getUserInput()` is a hypothetical function that gets user input. The `replace` function is used to remove any non-alphanumeric characters from the user input before it is used in the regular expression.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using jQuery, so jQuery would be a required library dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP ReDoS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)\n\nPlease note that the links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "daf6a125-94b7-3e32-9d4d-9be7d3840013",
              "name": "\"Logging Errors for Debugging and Security Monitoring in Empty Catch Block\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, an empty catch block vulnerability occurs when an exception is caught but not handled properly. This can lead to unexpected behavior, making the application prone to bugs and security issues. In the provided code snippet, the exception is caught but nothing is done with it, which means any error that occurs will be silently ignored. This can make debugging difficult as no error messages would be logged, and it can also pose a security risk as malicious activities could go unnoticed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to at least log the error in the catch block. This can be done using `console.error()` or a logging library. This will help in debugging by providing information about what went wrong. For security monitoring, it is recommended to use a security monitoring tool that can alert when exceptions occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nb.ready);var j=false;try{j=E.frameElement==null}catch(s){console.error(s)}t.documentElement.doScroll&&j&&a()}}},isFunction:function(j){return b.type(j)===\"function\"},isArray:Array.isArray||function(j){return b.type(j)===\"array\"},isWindow:function(j){return j&&typeof j===\"object\"&&\"setInterval\"in j},isNaN:function(j){return j==null||!r.test(j)||isNaN(j)},type:function(j){return j==null?String(j):R[y.call(j)]||\"object\"},isPlainObject:function(j){if(!j||b.type(j)!==\"object\"||j.nodeType||b.isWindow(j))return false;if(j.constructor&&\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using jQuery, but it's hard to tell without more context. \n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-390: Detection of Error Condition Without Action](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/390.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-390",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/390.html"
                  }
